  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, defined as the concentration of probability on favorable outcomes. In this context, the higher probability of a nonzero outcome in Lottery A (25% vs. 20%) makes it more left-skewed, which aligns with the reason’s preference. The individual also focuses on the higher chance of winning in Lottery A and justifies the choice based on this increased probability, even with a lower amount. Although the term 'skewness' is not used, the individual is effectively responding to the same informational structure—the relative probability mass on favorable outcomes. The comparison of probabilities of nonzero outcomes and the preference for a higher chance of winning despite lower payoff align closely with the logic of preferring more left-skewed distributions. Therefore, the individual’s reasoning mirrors the decision reason, even if not articulated in statistical terms.",
  "final_assessment_score": 95
}

